#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120705} :activity/id #uuid "0110b0af-3b1d-5486-8362-7c1bb8e6b885" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049685 :topic/id #uuid "0aaa911f-edc7-5c7d-a55c-6b4dae2d7006" :topic/math-academy-id 1773} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121046 :tutorial/content "One way to simplify a determinant calculation is to create a row (or column) containing all zeros except for one entry. Then, we can use a Laplace expansion across that row (or column) to compute the determinant.\n\nLet's demonstrate this method on the matrix $A$ below.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 14 & 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can make entries to the right of $a_{21}=1$ equal to zero using elementary *column* operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one column to another column in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 14 & 17\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -2 & -7\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -2 & -7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(21-10) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4a9a955-bf0a-5e47-973a-fa7d6e4191be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3240 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5e9a2059-bb14-516d-a920-81186f0ec60f" :step/math-academy-id 17785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119870 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119687 :question/id #uuid "85acf106-2f03-5509-8ad0-08